WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.880
The first film showed a sealed room filling with what it could not clear.

2
00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:06.460
Walls can trap trouble.

3
00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:08.900
They can also keep it away.

4
00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:15.140
Your cells make new proteins, and machinery that destroys proteins, in one small

5
00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:19.580
space. How does a new protein reach the surface unharmed?

6
00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:25.450
Follow one protein the cell will export.

7
00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:28.030
It is made at the rough ER.

8
00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:34.530
Then it rides a conveyor belt of sealed rooms: ER, then Golgi, then the cell

9
00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:39.610
surface. Along the way, it never passes through the cell's open workspace.

10
00:00:40.783 --> 00:00:42.523
Each room has its own tools.

11
00:00:43.803 --> 00:00:49.503
The ER interior has its own folding enzymes, and conditions that favor disulfide

12
00:00:49.503 --> 00:00:54.203
bonds. Most sugar chains are added there and in the Golgi.

13
00:00:55.583 --> 00:00:57.463
Misfolded proteins are held back.

14
00:00:58.743 --> 00:01:02.463
Those that cannot be fixed are pushed out to the cytosol, to be destroyed.

15
00:01:03.567 --> 00:01:06.587
That destroying machinery waits out in the cytosol.

16
00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:10.307
Properly folded cargo never meets it.

17
00:01:11.287 --> 00:01:16.307
At the surface, its bubble fuses with the outer membrane and releases the cargo

18
00:01:16.307 --> 00:01:21.227
outside. That is how a pancreatic beta cell releases insulin.

19
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:28.940
Here is the idea. A wall lets each room keep its own enzymes and its own conditions.

20
00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:34.700
So the cell decides what reaches each room, what it holds, and how it is kept.

21
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:41.440
The lysosome's digestive enzymes are made at the rough ER too, then addressed to the

22
00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:45.380
lysosome. Suppose one escapes into the cytosol.

23
00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:48.140
Does it work as well out there?

24
00:01:54.067 --> 00:02:00.647
Much less well. These enzymes work best in acid, and poorly at the cytosol's pH.

25
00:02:01.787 --> 00:02:04.047
So one that escapes is far less active.

26
00:02:05.427 --> 00:02:07.327
Still, a large leak can kill a cell.

27
00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:09.990
That acid is one lock.

28
00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:15.230
Inside a lysosome, the pH sits near 4.5 to 5.

29
00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:18.610
The cytosol sits near 7.2.

30
00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:22.070
Each pH unit is a tenfold step.

31
00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:28.250
So the lysosome holds a few hundred times the free hydrogen ion concentration.

32
00:02:29.533 --> 00:02:31.333
The other lock is the membrane.

33
00:02:32.613 --> 00:02:34.113
The enzymes cannot get out.

34
00:02:35.373 --> 00:02:37.913
Their targets get in only by routes the cell builds.

35
00:02:39.113 --> 00:02:41.053
Two locks guard one dangerous room.

36
00:02:42.067 --> 00:02:46.067
So one cell can build proteins and digest them, without a clash.

37
00:02:47.347 --> 00:02:50.007
The new protein rides sealed rooms to the surface.

38
00:02:51.307 --> 00:02:54.747
The digesting enzymes stay behind membrane and acid.

39
00:02:56.207 --> 00:02:57.547
Walls do more than trap trouble.

40
00:02:58.587 --> 00:03:00.527
They decide where each job can happen.
